\label{le:norm_Gauss}
	Given parameter $d$ and $\delta$, there exists an explicitly computable $D := D(d, \delta)$ such that the following holds:\\
	Let $({\bm u}_1,\ldots,{\bm u}_d, {\bm v}_1,\ldots, {\bm v}_d)$ be a multivariate Gaussian distribution such that,
	\begin{itemize}
	\item each ${\bm u}_i,{\bm v}_i \in {\mathbb R}^D$ are marginally distributed as $D$-dimensional standard Gaussians, i.e. $\gamma_D$.
	\item for each $j \in [D]$, the joint distribution of the $j$-th coordinates, i.e., $(u_{1,j}, \ldots, u_{d,j}, v_{1,j}, \ldots, v_{d,j})$, is independent across different values of $j$.
	\end{itemize}
	Then,
	$$\inabs{\mathbb{E}\limits_{\set{{\bm u}_i, {\bm v}_i}_i} \ \insquare{\prod_{i=1}^d \frac{\inangle{{\bm u}_i, {\bm v}_i}}{\norm{2}{{\bm u}_i} \norm{2}{{\bm v}_i}}} - \prod_{i=1}^d \ \mathbb{E}\limits_{\set{{\bm u}_i, {\bm v}_i}_i} \ \insquare{\frac{\inangle{{\bm u}_i, {\bm v}_i}}{D}}} ~\le~ \delta.$$
	In particular, one may take $D = \frac{d^{O(d)}}{\delta^2}$.

